# Merge Adjacent Equal Elements

**Problem:** [LeetCode – Merge Adjacent Equal Elements](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-adjacent-equal-elements/)

## Approach: Stack

### Theory
We use a vector as a stack to maintain the resulting elements. For each element in the input array, compare it with the last element of the result vector.

- If both elements are equal, remove the last element and double the current element.
- Continue merging while the last element equals the updated value.
- If the elements are different, push the current value into the result vector.

This process ensures that adjacent equal elements are merged repeatedly until no further merge is possible.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<long long> mergeAdjacent(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<long long> ans;

        for (int num : nums) {
            long long pivot = num;

            while (!ans.empty() && ans.back() == pivot) {
                ans.pop_back();
                pivot *= 2;
            }

            ans.push_back(pivot);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), because each element is pushed into and removed from the stack at most once.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n)\), for storing the resulting elements in the vector.

## Key Learnings

- Using a vector as a stack with `push_back()`, `back()`, and `pop_back()`.
- Applying greedy merging to adjacent equal elements.
- Handling cascading merges efficiently.
- Using `long long` to avoid integer overflow when values are doubled.
